TY  - JOUR
ID  - 43065563
TI  - Next-generation anti-obesity drugs: emerging molecular targets and translational opportunities.
AU  - Shetty Sneha
AU  - Rangle Asmi
AU  - Mukherjee Anirban Goutam
AU  - Bharti Ankit Kumar
AU  - Jena Sudip Prasad
AU  - Prince Sabina Evan
AU  - Gopalakrishnan Abilash Valsala
JO  - Journal of diabetes and metabolic disorders
PY  - 2027
DO  - 10.1007/s40200-026-02101-4
AB  - Obesity is a heterogeneous, chronic metabolic disease driven by dysregulation of energy homeostasis across interconnected central nervous system, gastrointestinal, adipose, endocrine, immune, and mitochondrial networks. Despite the established roles of lifestyle intervention and metabolic/bariatric surgery, limitations in accessibility, durability, and interindividual response have stimulated the development of mechanism-based pharmacotherapies. This review critically examines emerging therapeutic strategies targeting integrated energy-regulatory pathways and evaluates their translational potential for precision obesity management. Current evidence was synthesized across major biological systems governing appetite, energy expenditure, and metabolic adaptation. Central melanocortin, Neuropeptide Y (NPY)/ Agouti-related peptide (AgRP), serotonergic, dopaminergic, and endocannabinoid pathways were considered alongside gut-derived incretin, amylin, Peptide YY (PYY), ghrelin, Fibroblast Growth Factor (FGF), and bile acid signaling. Emerging approaches targeting adipose remodeling, thermogenesis, mitochondrial energetics, AMPK-mTOR-sirtuin signaling, inflammation, and fibrosis were also evaluated. Genetic, epigenetic, and artificial intelligence-based approaches to patient stratification were incorporated to assess prospects for precision therapy. Obesity pharmacotherapy is shifting from conventional appetite suppression toward coordinated modulation of interconnected metabolic pathways. Incretin-based and multi-agonist therapies provide the strongest clinical evidence for substantial weight reduction and metabolic improvement. However, treatment discontinuation, weight regain, adverse effects, cost, and heterogeneous therapeutic responses remain major challenges. Strategies targeting adipose tissue dysfunction, mitochondrial metabolism, thermogenesis, immune-metabolic signaling, and fibrosis offer additional opportunities for disease modification, although many remain in preclinical or early clinical development. Increasing evidence for biological heterogeneity supports individualized therapeutic selection. The emerging therapeutic paradigm positions obesity as a systems-level disorder requiring mechanism-informed, individualized intervention. Integrating CNS-gut-adipose-mitochondrial biology with multi-omics, biomarkers, and artificial intelligence may enable more durable and precise treatment. Long-term safety, durability, affordability, equitable access, and clinical validation remain essential for translation.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43065563/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42946204
TI  - The GLP-1 receptor agonist semaglutide reduces alcohol consumption and anxiety-like behaviors during withdrawal in Marchigian Sardinian alcohol-preferring rats.
AU  - Palmisano Michela
AU  - Mrizak Hela
AU  - Gonzalez Angela E
AU  - St Onge Celsey M
AU  - Erikson Chloe M
AU  - Cruz Bryan
AU  - Flores-Bonilla Annabelle
AU  - Vlkolinsky Roman
AU  - Sharlai Oleksandr
AU  - Benedict Jessica
AU  - Bajo Michal
AU  - Leggio Lorenzo
AU  - Ciccocioppo Roberto
AU  - Roberto Marisa
JO  - Neuropharmacology
PY  - 2027
DO  - 10.1016/j.neuropharm.2026.111240
AB  - Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) receptor agonists have emerged as promising pharmacotherapeutics for alcohol use disorder (AUD). Here, we examined the effects of semaglutide, a long-acting GLP-1 receptor agonist, on alcohol intake and anxiety-related behaviors in Marchigian Sardinian alcohol-preferring rats, a model characterized by excessive alcohol intake and pronounced anxious and depressive-like phenotypes. Male and female rats were treated with semaglutide (0.001, 0.01 and 0.1 mg/kg, s.c.) and vehicle. Voluntary alcohol consumption was assessed using the two-bottle choice paradigm. Locomotion, anxiety, and stress responses were evaluated during withdrawal and in naïve rats, following administration of the highest dose of semaglutide (0.1 mg/kg). Furthermore, the acute effects of semaglutide on GABAergic transmission were assessed in the central nucleus of the amygdala and in the infralimbic (IL) medial pre-frontal cortex (mPFC) of naïve rats. Semaglutide reduced voluntary alcohol consumption in a dose-dependent manner, and it produced anxiolytic-like behaviors during withdrawal in the open field test. In naïve animals, semaglutide reduced locomotor activity, lowered blood glucose levels, and decreased fat mass and GABAergic transmission in the IL mPFC. Our findings support the use of the GLP-1 receptor agonist semaglutide as a promising pharmacotherapy for the treatment of AUD and its associated psychiatric symptoms.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42946204/
