ID  - 43068476
TI  - Amylin-Based Pharmacotherapy for Type 2 Diabetes in the Incretin Era: Mechanisms, Emerging Evidence, and Clinical Implementation.
AU  - Varghese Ron T
AU  - Calcaterra Giuseppe
AU  - Fanaropoulou Nina M
AU  - Varghese Diana G
AU  - Iacobellis Gianluca
JO  - Endocrinology, diabetes & metabolism
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1002/edm2.70401
AB  - Type 2 diabetes (T2D) management increasingly requires simultaneous improvement in glycaemia, excess adiposity, treatment burden, and cardiovascular and kidney risk. Amylin slows gastric emptying, suppresses postprandial glucagon secretion, and promotes satiation, providing a complementary therapeutic pathway to incretin-based treatment. We conducted a targeted narrative search of PubMed/MEDLINE and trial registries through 30 September 2026. We prioritised peer-reviewed Phase 2 and Phase 3 human trials and treated registry records as planned rather than completed evidence. Pramlintide established the clinical value of amylin replacement but remains limited by mealtime administration, gastrointestinal adverse effects, and the need for insulin adjustment. Long-acting amylin analogues and amylin-incretin combinations have renewed interest in this pathway. In a 32-week Phase 2 T2D trial, CagriSema reduced HbA1c by 2.2 percentage points and body weight by 15.6%; HbA1c superiority over semaglutide was not demonstrated (estimated difference -0.4 percentage points, 95% CI -0.8 to 0.0; p = 0.075). Phase 3 trials have evaluated early T2D, oral-therapy-treated T2D, and basal-insulin-treated T2D, although populations, comparators, durations, and estimands differ. Gastrointestinal tolerability and treatment discontinuation remain important considerations, and cardiovascular and kidney benefits have not been established. Amylin-based therapies could broaden metabolic treatment for T2D, but their clinical role will depend on comparative efficacy, tolerability, persistence, body composition, patient-reported outcomes, and long-term cardiovascular and kidney outcomes.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43068476/
