AB  - Preparing for a colonoscopy involves bowel cleansing and medication adjustments, including anticoagulants and anti-diabetics. Various protocols can make these adjustments confusing for both physicians and patients. While essential for a successful procedure, this preparation can cause discomfort and carry potential risks. We aimed to provide clear guidance for colonoscopy preparation, achieving consensus among multiple societies to ensure safety and effectiveness, including patient forms. This position paper is based on guidelines from major professional associations in Europe and the USA. Representatives from associations in Gastroenterology, Pharmacology, Hematology, Nephrology, Endocrinology, and Geriatrics have collaborated to develop these recommendations. Bowel preparation now requires two days using a split-dose method. Updated guidelines include "enhanced" preparation definitions, adjustments for anti-diabetic drugs, discontinuation of anticoagulants, enoxaparin bridging, and resuming therapy post-procedure. PicoLax is suitable for patients with a GFR above 30 mL/min. New glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) receptor agonist guidelines address gastroparesis and sedation risks. Finally, we provide breastfeeding recommendations for colonoscopy. This consensus paper provides interdisciplinary guidance to ensure effective bowel preparation while addressing safety concerns, making it a valuable resource for healthcare providers and patients.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43068364/
