ID  - 43066155
TI  - Acute Colonic Pseudo-obstruction Requiring Subtotal Colectomy Following Semaglutide Use.
AU  - Loh Hanyou
AU  - Aiti Diana
AU  - Bolyard Jennifer
JO  - ACG case reports journal
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.14309/crj.0000000000002393
AB  - Glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists are increasingly used for obesity and diabetes management and are commonly associated with mild gastrointestinal adverse effects. Severe dysmotility is rare. We report a 61-year-old man with class III obesity and multiple comorbidities who developed acute colonic pseudo-obstruction following once-weekly injectable semaglutide, ultimately requiring subtotal colectomy. Despite discontinuation of therapy and conservative management including bowel rest and colonoscopic decompression, colonic dilatation progressed to 13 cm without obstruction, prompting surgical intervention given perforation risk. This case highlights a rare but severe gastrointestinal event associated with glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists, underscoring the importance of careful patient selection.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43066155/
