ID  - 43063617
TI  - Beyond weight loss: GLP-1-based therapies and the reversal of type 2 diabetes pathophysiology.
AU  - Rossatto Thales
AU  - de Andrade Oliveira Ricardo
AU  - Manica Aline
AU  - Cardoso Andréia Machado
JO  - Journal of diabetes and its complications
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1016/j.jdiacomp.2026.109453
AB  - Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1)-based therapies have transformed the treatment of type 2 diabetes (T2D), producing substantial improvements in glycemic control and, with newer agents, unprecedented reductions in body weight. However, their effects extend beyond these conventional outcomes and include reductions in visceral and hepatic fat, improved insulin sensitivity, attenuation of glucolipotoxicity, and enhancement of β-cell function. These actions overlap with mechanisms implicated in the reversal of T2D following substantial weight loss, raising the possibility that GLP-1-based therapies may modify the underlying pathophysiology of the disease rather than simply control its metabolic manifestations. In this review, we integrate evidence linking ectopic fat accumulation, hepatic insulin resistance, altered lipid trafficking, oxidative and inflammatory stress, and β-cell dysfunction with the development and reversibility of T2D. We then examine how GLP-1 receptor agonists and dual GIP/GLP-1 receptor agonism remodel these metabolic and cellular abnormalities and discuss whether such changes may facilitate β-cell functional recovery. Particular attention is given to the distinction between treatment-induced normoglycemia and medication-free remission. Although current evidence indicates that GLP-1-based therapies can profoundly modify the diabetic metabolic phenotype, durability after treatment withdrawal remains uncertain. We propose that the potential for disease modification may depend on the interaction between the magnitude of metabolic unloading and residual β-cell functional reserve. Prospective withdrawal studies incorporating detailed assessments of β-cell function, insulin sensitivity, and ectopic adiposity are needed to determine whether early and profound incretin-based intervention can translate metabolic normalization into durable, medication-independent remission of T2D.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43063617/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 43063014
TI  - Where patients obtain GLP-1 receptor agonists: direct-to-consumer channels, compounded product use, and reason-specific nonadherence in a national survey.
AU  - Bhagavathula Akshaya Srikanth
AU  - Abegaz Tadesse M
JO  - American journal of health-system pharmacy : AJHP : official journal of the American Society of Health-System Pharmacists
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1093/ajhp/zxag415
AB  - To examine acquisition channels for glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1 RAs) and associations with compounded use, prescription status, and nonadherence. This cross-sectional analysis used the National Center for Health Statistics Rapid Surveys System Round 7 (July-August 2025; N = 8118). Among 976 past-year users, channels were classified as clinician/pharmacy without direct-to-consumer (DTC) acquisition, any DTC telehealth/medical spa, or other. Survey-weighted logistic regression estimated adjusted odds ratios (aORs). GLP-1 RA use was reported by 9.8% of adults, approximately 25.9 million. Of users, 18.3% used a DTC or medical spa channel and 9.2% reported no clinician prescription. Among users reporting known compounded status, compounded use was 73.0% (95% CI, 62.7%-81.2%) for DTC versus 12.9% (8.8%-18.4%) for conventional acquisition (aOR, 14.56; 95% CI, 7.99-26.54). Overall, 22.6% reported compounded use and 8.6% did not know. DTC acquisition was associated with cost-attributed nonadherence (aOR, 4.94; 95% CI, 2.77-8.81); the supply-attributed association was not statistically significant (aOR, 1.15; 95% CI, 0.56-2.38; contrast P < 0.001). Associations were also observed for side-effect-attributed reduction/discontinuation (aOR, 2.12; 95% CI, 1.05-4.27) and complete discontinuation (aOR, 1.83; 95% CI, 1.01-3.32). Counseling associations were imprecise. Insurance nonpayment was associated with DTC acquisition (aOR, 8.01; 95% CI, 4.70-13.67). DTC acquisition was associated with compounded use, insurance nonpayment, and cost-attributed nonadherence. These cross-sectional findings suggest that acquisition sources and reasons for nonadherence may be useful topics for patient assessment. They do not establish causal effects of acquisition channel or demonstrate that medication records are incomplete.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43063014/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 43053482
TI  - When Clinical Innovation Outpaces Implementation: A Dual Diffusion Model for GLP-1 Therapy and Employer Cardiometabolic Health.
AU  - Halterman Steven L
JO  - Population health management
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1177/19427891261507912
AB  - Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1)-based and related incretin therapies have rapidly expanded from glycemic management to obesity and broader cardiovascular-kidney-metabolic care. Their population-health impact, however, depends on more than therapeutic efficacy. Translation into durable benefit requires clinicians to identify and manage appropriate patients, benefit systems to enable effective access, and patients to initiate and sustain treatment. This review applies diffusion of innovations theory to propose a dual diffusion model for GLP-1 therapy and employer cardiometabolic health. The model conceptualizes provider implementation and employer/plan effective access as interdependent organizational diffusion processes that converge on the patient treatment trajectory. Their interaction produces 4 implementation states: implementation failure, identification gap, access gap, and integrated opportunity. Employer/plan effective access is proposed as a construct extending beyond binary coverage to include eligibility, affordability, utilization management, provider access, administrative feasibility, continuity, and treatment-support infrastructure. The model further distinguishes treatment initiation from sustainability and introduces system-generated discontinuation as a conceptual description of treatment cessation driven by structural barriers. Clinically Integrated cardiometabolic optimization is proposed as an implementation architecture positioning pharmacotherapy within coordinated longitudinal management of cardiometabolic risk rather than as an isolated pharmacy intervention. Seven propositions define a research agenda for testing the model. As incretin therapies expand across cardiometabolic indications, closing the efficacy-to-implementation gap may become as consequential as further gains in pharmacologic efficacy.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43053482/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 43047647
TI  - Case Report: Resolution of severe hypertriglyceridemia and cessation of exogenous insulin during ketogenic-range therapeutic carbohydrate reduction, insulin deprescribing, and tirzepatide.
AU  - Calkins Matthew
AU  - Buchanan Laura
AU  - Dudzinski Austin
AU  - Wageman Joshua
JO  - Frontiers in nutrition
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.3389/fnut.2026.1960774
AB  - Severe hypertriglyceridemia [sHTG; triglycerides ≥500 mg/dL (SI: ≥5.65 mmol/L)] is conventionally managed with dietary fat restriction, but in insulin-resistant type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) low-fat diets may increase carbohydrate intake and worsen dysglycemia. A 51-year-old woman with persistent sHTG, five episodes of pancreatitis, T2DM requiring 210 units of insulin daily, and a body mass index of 27.5 kg/m2 was evaluated. Despite two decades of low-fat counseling and multidrug therapy, fasting triglycerides were 860 mg/dL (SI: 9.71 mmol/L), hemoglobin A1c 7.1% (SI: 54 mmol/mol), glucose management indicator (GMI) 8.3%, and continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) time in range (TIR) 35%. She was prescribed therapeutic carbohydrate reduction (TCR), a whole-food eating pattern delivered as a written food list designed to provide fewer than 20 g/day of carbohydrate (approximately 5% of energy, within the ketogenic range); intake was not quantified and ketones were not measured. A chylomicronemia gene panel identified no pathogenic variants, consistent with probable multifactorial chylomicronemia syndrome (MCS). At 1 month, after initiation of carbohydrate reduction with concurrent CGM-guided reduction of insulin from 210 to 20 units/day, with no other medication change and before tirzepatide, triglycerides had fallen 49.5% to 434 mg/dL (SI: 4.90 mmol/L), GMI to 7.1%, and TIR had risen to 74%. This brought triglycerides below the level at which endocrinology, cardiology, and the treating clinician had, by individualized judgment, deferred incretin therapy, and tirzepatide 2.5 mg was initiated. At 5 months triglycerides were 162 mg/dL (SI: 1.83 mmol/L), hemoglobin A1c 6.1% (SI: 43 mmol/mol), body weight had fallen 18%, and exogenous insulin had been discontinued. At 9 months she remained free of exogenous insulin, with triglycerides 172 mg/dL (SI: 1.94 mmol/L) and hemoglobin A1c 6.1% (SI: 43 mmol/mol). The first-month triglyceride reduction occurred during ketogenic-range TCR with concurrent insulin reduction; later improvements occurred during continued TCR, insulin cessation, weight loss, and tirzepatide, and the contributions of these components cannot be separated. In selected patients with probable MCS and insulin-resistant T2DM, supervised ketogenic-range TCR may be considered when low-fat counseling has not achieved sustained control and may permit reconsideration of therapies deferred by individualized judgment for pancreatitis risk.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43047647/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 43046651
TI  - Painful Non-Diabetic Lumbosacral Radiculoplexus Neuropathy Following Tirzepatide Exposure: A Case Report.
AU  - Stabile Liberato
AU  - Poli Loris
AU  - Leggio Ugo
AU  - Bozzoni Virginia
AU  - Bissolotti Luciano
AU  - Gasparotti Roberto
AU  - Pilotto Andrea
AU  - Padovani Alessandro
AU  - Filosto Massimiliano
JO  - Journal of the peripheral nervous system : JPNS
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1111/jns.70188
AB  - Lumbosacral radiculoplexus neuropathy (LRPN) is an uncommon neuropathic syndrome occurring predominantly in patients with diabetes mellitus, although non-diabetic forms are increasingly recognized. Rare peripheral neuropathies have been reported during glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonist (GLP-1 receptor agonist [GLP-1RA]) therapy; however, LRPN has not previously been described in this clinical context, particularly in non-diabetic individuals. We describe a well-characterized case of painful non-diabetic LRPN developing following a period of rapid metabolic shift during tirzepatide therapy. A 62-year-old man previously treated with tirzepatide for weight loss developed, approximately 8 weeks after discontinuation, acute left-sided low back pain, rapidly progressing to severe neuropathic pain, distal weakness, and sensory loss. MR neurography demonstrated enlargement and contrast enhancement of the left L5 nerve root and sciatic nerve, associated with denervation edema. Electrophysiological studies demonstrated an axonal process consistent with lumbosacral radiculoplexus neuropathy, while cerebrospinal fluid analysis showed albuminocytological dissociation. Classical diabetic-related plexopathy was excluded by normal glucose values; an extensive work-up excluded infectious, neoplastic, paraneoplastic, and systemic inflammatory causes. The patient was treated with intravenous methylprednisolone followed by an oral corticosteroid taper and intensive rehabilitation. At 8-month follow-up, neuropathic pain had completely resolved and plantar flexion had recovered, whereas severe foot dorsiflexion weakness persisted, and the patient walked with a single crutch and an ankle-foot orthosis, consistent with the expected outcome of severe axonal lumbosacral radiculoplexus neuropathy. Tirzepatide exposure was associated with rapid weight loss in a normoglycaemic individual. This case highlights a severe non-diabetic lumbosacral radiculoplexus neuropathy following a period of rapid metabolic shift during GLP-1RA therapy. Although causality cannot be established, the temporal association and clinical context raise the hypothesis that abrupt metabolic changes may act as a permissive factor for immune-mediated lumbosacral plexus injury in susceptible individuals.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43046651/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 43044077
TI  - Optimizing use of GLP-1 receptor agonists: safety, tolerability, adherence, and patient selection in cardiovascular practice.
AU  - Pejcic Ana V
AU  - Stefanovic Srdjan M
AU  - Milosavljevic Milos N
JO  - Journal of cardiovascular pharmacology
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1097/FJC.0000000000001901
AB  - Glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1RAs) have fundamentally transformed modern cardiometabolic care, moving beyond glycemic control to become a cornerstone of obesity management and potent cardiovascular risk reduction. Large-scale clinical trials consistently highlight their cardioprotective benefits, demonstrating significant reductions in major adverse cardiovascular events (MACE), cardiovascular mortality, and obesity-related heart failure complications. However, translating these remarkable clinical trial successes into routine clinical practice reveals a marked gap between efficacy and effectiveness, primarily driven by suboptimal patient persistence and adherence. This narrative review synthesizes current evidence regarding the pharmacology, safety, tolerability, adherence, and patient selection of GLP-1RAs to support optimal therapeutic decision-making in cardiovascular practice. While long-term data disprove clear causal relationships with pancreatic or thyroid malignancies, a high incidence of predictable, dose-related gastrointestinal adverse events (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea) remains the leading cause of early treatment discontinuation. Additionally, treatment persistence in clinical practice is heavily influenced by financial burdens, supply chain instability, and formulation choices. Cardioprotective benefits are well-established for patients with type 2 diabetes and atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease. Mitigating challenges in clinical practice requires a systematic, multi-step prescribing framework focused on targeted patient selection, gradual individualized dose titration ("start low and go slow"), and proactive multidisciplinary symptom management.
