TI  - Evaluation of semaglutide in an ex vivo human placental perfusion model to estimate maternal-fetal transfer.
AU  - Chan Eddie
AU  - Zabek Magdalene
AU  - Balluku Alice
AU  - Garcia-Bournissen Facundo
AU  - Hutson Janine R
JO  - Placenta
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1016/j.placenta.2026.11.007
AB  - Semaglutide, a glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonist, is increasingly used in females of reproductive age for glycemic control and weight management, with emerging evidence suggesting benefits on fertility and obstetrical outcomes. However, pregnancy safety data remain limited, and current guidance advises discontinuation before conception due to uncertain fetal exposure risk. Since many pregnancies are unplanned, inadvertent exposures are inevitable-further data is needed to inform clinical counselling and risk assessment. This study aims to determine whether semaglutide crosses the human placenta using an ex vivo dual-perfused cotyledon model. Transplacental transfer of semaglutide was assessed at clinically relevant (222 ng/mL, n = 4) or at ten-fold higher (2220 ng/mL, n = 4) concentrations. Each perfusion included a 1-h control period followed by a 3-h experimental period with drug exposure. Perfusates samples were collected over time to measure semaglutide concentrations and quality control markers. Semaglutide was quantified via a validated enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay kit. Paired Student's t-tests were used, with significance defined as p < 0.05. Placental viability was maintained throughout, supported by stable metabolic parameters and appropriate antipyrine transfer, confirming intact and functional cotyledons. Maternal semaglutide concentrations remained stable over the 180-min perfusion period in both conditions, with no significant time-dependent changes (p > 0.05). Fetal perfusate concentrations were all below the lower limit of quantification. Semaglutide demonstrated minimal-to-no detectable transfer across the term human placenta, even at supratherapeutic concentrations. These findings suggest limited placental passage during term gestation and offer guidance for decision making regarding semaglutide use during pregnancy.
