TY  - JOUR
ID  - 43011373
TI  - Fifty years at the addiction bench and bedside: from withdrawal syndromes to adaptive brain states.
AU  - Gold Mark S
JO  - Frontiers in public health
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.3389/fpubh.2026.1969403
AB  - Over the past half-century, addiction medicine has moved from moral and symptom-based explanations toward a neuroscience of adaptation, learning, motivation, and recovery. This Perspective uses several linked translational programs-opioid withdrawal and the locus coeruleus, cocaine dependence and dopamine, environmental drug exposure, physician health, and food reward-to illustrate how observations at the bedside can generate mechanistic studies and how laboratory findings can, in turn, change treatment and public health. LC stimulation produced signs and symptoms that looked to me like opioid withdrawal. So, we thought that noradrenergic hyperactivity in opioid withdrawal caused symptoms, and while methadone or morphine could treat these, we could also treat them with a non-opioid, clonidine. Earlier noradrenergic interventions had been attempted with limited clinical success. Our work provided the first hypothesis-driven, double-blind, placebo-controlled demonstration that a non-opioid medication could suppress the acute human opioid withdrawal syndrome. This mechanism-based treatment was greeted with considerable interest because some clinicians had assumed that any medication capable of reversing opioid withdrawal distress must itself be an opioid. Clonidine and later lofexidine did just that and facilitated transition to naltrexone. Recognition that cocaine could produce compulsive use without a dramatic physical withdrawal syndrome helped shift the field toward reward circuitry, craving, pathological attraction, anhedonia, and relapse. Studies of tobacco, cannabis, and opioid smoke broadened prevention by showing that involuntary second- and third-hand exposure can be biologically detected and, most clearly for tobacco, can produce biologically meaningful effects. Third-hand exposure refers to residual drug-related contaminants that persist on indoor surfaces, dust, clothing, or other materials after active smoking has ended. Contemporary work on neuromodulation, exercise, psychedelics, and GLP-1 metabolic-reward pathways extends the same translational logic. Addiction is best understood as an acquired adaptive brain state supported by anti-survival or bad learning, development, and environment; recovery is active neurobehavioral relearning, not simply detoxification or drug clearance. Public health should therefore join brain sciences, work to delay initiation, identify emerging risk before a severe substance use disorder develops, prevent involuntary exposure, and provide rapid access to mechanism-based treatment, recovery-supportive environments, and outcomes measured over 5 years or longer.
