AB  - The increasing use of GLP-1 receptor agonizts for weight loss has resulted in a growing population of patients seeking abdominoplasty; however, evidence-based guidelines for perioperative management of these medications are lacking. To compare postoperative outcomes among three perioperative GLP-1 analog management protocols, a protocol with at least 2-week preoperative cessation and delayed resumption (Group A), preoperative cessation with early postoperative resumption (Group B), and short preoperative cessation with no postoperative resumption (Group C) were employed. This multicenter retrospective study included 91 female patients who underwent abdominoplasty following GLP-1-induced weight loss (Group A: n=29; Group B: n=35; and Group C: n=27). Patients were stratified based on actual perioperative GLP-1 management. Outcomes included body mass index (BMI) changes, drain output, complications, and hemoglobin levels over a 12-month follow-up. Non-parametric tests were employed for statistical analysis. Group A demonstrated superior BMI reduction (18.84 ± 3.89 kg/m²) with continued loss between 6-12 months (1.13 ± 1.39 kg/m²). Group C demonstrated significant weight relapse during 6-12 months (-1.03 ± 0.85 kg/m², p<0.001). Group B exhibited the highest drain output (900 mL, p=0.004) and lowest early hemoglobin (11.3 g/dL, p<0.001). Short-term complication rates differed significantly among the groups (p=0.014), being the highest in Group B (24/35). Long-term complication rates were comparable across the groups (p>0.05). A perioperative GLP-1 management protocol with adequate preoperative cessation and delayed postoperative resumption was associated with superior weight outcomes. Although long-term complication rates were comparable, early resumption was associated with a higher rate of short-term complications. Level III.
