AB  - To evaluate the clinical feasibility of a low-dose tirzepatide strategy in older adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus in routine outpatient practice. This single-center retrospective cohort included Japanese adults who initiated tirzepatide between April 2024 and December 2025 and received a maximum observed dose of ≤5 mg/wk through week 28. The primary end point in adults aged ≥65 years combined treatment persistence, absence of adverse event (AE)-related discontinuation, and either ≥3% weight loss or a glycated hemoglobin (A1C) reduction ≥1.0 percentage point (≥11 mmol/mol). Younger adults were contextual comparators using overlap weighting. Among 122 tirzepatide initiators, 107 were included in the low-dose strategy cohort, including 37 older adults. The primary end point was achieved by 23/37 (62.2%); 33/37 (89.2%) remained on treatment, and 2/37 (5.4%) discontinued because of AEs. Median body weight change was -3.07%, median A1C change was -1.0 percentage point (-11 mmol/mol), and 19/37 (51.4%) achieved A1c <7.0% (<53 mmol/mol). Renal, hydration, and nutritional laboratory markers remained generally stable. Overlap-weighted comparisons showed no clear age-related attenuation. In selected older adults, low-dose tirzepatide provided a modest metabolic benefit with high persistence and infrequent AE-related discontinuation.
